-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Negative Weights:** Raw optimized skinning weights can be negative; ReLU and normalization are required to prevent invalid blending [S2].
-*   **Pose Blend Shapes:** Using SMPL pose blend shapes before deformation can introduce inaccuracies; it is recommended to rely on the MLP for pose-dependent deformations instead [S2].
-*   **Jacobian Computation:** The Jacobian $J$ must include contributions from both the LBS term and the MLP term to correctly transform the covariance [G].
+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Degenerate Poses:** Extreme poses may cause skinning weights to produce invalid transformations. Guard against negative determinants in covariance update [G].
+*   **Precision:** Jacobian computation must be numerically stable. Use automatic differentiation rather than finite differences [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Linear Blend Skinning for Gaussian Splatting
-A specific implementation of the rigid deformation component where LBS is applied directly to canonical Gaussians using precomputed rigid joint transformations and learnable per-Gaussian skinning weights [S2]. It avoids pose blend shapes and template vertex deformation, relying on an MLP for pose-dependent deformations instead [S2]. It is chosen when computational efficiency in precomputing transforms is prioritized and when avoiding inaccuracies from SMPL blend shapes is critical [S2].
+### [Linear Blend Skinning for Gaussian Splatting](16258-linear-blend-skinning-for-gaussian-splatting.md)
+A specific deformation technique using skeleton-driven rigid joint transformations and learnable per-Gaussian skinning weights. It avoids pose blend shapes and template vertex deformation, relying on an MLP for pose-dependent deformations to handle shape estimation inaccuracies.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Deformation is $O(N \cdot N_{bones})$ for LBS plus $O(N)$ for the MLP. Precomputing transforms is $O(N_{poses} \cdot N_{bones})$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot (D_{pos} + D_{cov} + D_{sh} + N_{bones}))$ for storing Gaussians and skinning weights.
-*   **Strengths:** Enables real-time rendering of animatable avatars; maintains structural integrity through parametric priors; handles large articulations via LBS [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Accuracy depends on the quality of the SMPL model and skinning weights; non-rigid details (muscle jiggles) require a capable deformation network [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Full-body human avatars requiring animation in novel poses [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** Head-centric avatars requiring fine facial details (use [Gaussian Avatar](1479-gaussian-avatar.md) or similar) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Deformation is $O(N \cdot N_{bones})$ for LBS plus $O(N)$ for the deformation network.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for Gaussian parameters. Additional memory for SMPL model and deformation network weights.
+*   **Performance:** Fast training and rendering compared to NeRF-based methods due to explicit Gaussian representation [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High fidelity for dynamic full-body avatars; real-time rendering capability; editability of Gaussians [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate SMPL fitting; non-rigid deformation field may struggle with extreme articulations or clothing changes [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time or near-real-time dynamic full-body avatars are needed from multi-view video.
+*   **When not to use:** For head-centric avatars with fine facial details (use [Gaussian Avatar](1479-gaussian-avatar.md)) or static scenes (use standard [3D Gaussian Splatting](52-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)).
 
 ## Background
-Full-body avatar modeling using 3D Gaussians has emerged as a powerful tool for VR and social applications, advancing along two main directions: full-body and head-centric modeling [S1]. The hybrid deformation strategy, combining LBS and pose-conditioned fields, was introduced to handle the complex dynamics of full-body motion [S1]. Key works include "Human Gaussian Splatting" which details the LBS implementation for Gaussians [S2], and broader surveys categorizing these methods [S1].
+Full-body Avatar Modeling using 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a key direction in 3D GS applications, alongside head-centric modeling [S1]. It leverages the speed and editability of GS to enable fast training and rendering of dynamic humans [S1]. The approach typically anchors Gaussians in canonical space and uses a hybrid deformation strategy combining LBS for rigid skeletal transformations and pose-conditioned deformation fields for non-rigid effects [S1]. This method is part of the broader trend of using parametric body models (like SMPL) to guide Gaussian deformation for animatable avatars [S1, 1477-gaussian-avatars.md].
 
